import{_ as o,o as s,c as t,a6 as a}from"./chunks/framework.DIp0zHSV.js";const m=JSON.parse('{"title":"Testing","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"development/testing.md","filePath":"development/testing.md","lastUpdated":1794278866000}'),d={name:"development/testing.md"};function r(c,e,n,i,l,h){return s(),t("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[a('<h1 id="testing" tabindex="-1">Testing <a class="header-anchor" href="#testing" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Testing&quot;">​</a></h1><p>LCARdS has five test layers, from fast node tests to smoke tests against a real Home Assistant. This page says what each layer is for, how it is set up and which one a change needs. The commands, what CI runs and the run-one-file recipe are in the maintainer cookbook: <a href="https://github.com/snootched/lcards/blob/main/COOKBOOK.md#tests" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">COOKBOOK.md: Tests</a>, <a href="https://github.com/snootched/lcards/blob/main/COOKBOOK.md#npm-scripts" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">npm scripts</a> and <a href="https://github.com/snootched/lcards/blob/main/COOKBOOK.md#ci-workflows" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">CI workflows</a>.</p><p><strong>Rule of thumb: every fix and every new behaviour gets a test in the layer that can see it</strong> (COOKBOOK.md, Tests). A console check or a screenshot you looked at once is not a test.</p><hr><h2 id="which-layer-for-what" tabindex="-1">Which Layer for What <a class="header-anchor" href="#which-layer-for-what" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Which Layer for What&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Layer</th><th>Runs</th><th>Use it for</th><th>Not for</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href="#node-tests">Node tests</a></td><td>CI and local, <code>npm test</code></td><td>Pure logic: config processing, schemas and docs checks, routing, templates, colour resolution, Python rules run from node, source guards</td><td>Anything that needs layout, a real Lit render or a browser API</td></tr><tr><td><a href="#layer-a-browser-components">Layer A: browser components</a></td><td>CI and local, <code>npm run test:browser</code></td><td>A real card from the built bundle in Chromium against a fake <code>hass</code>: rendering, state updates, actions, sizing, editors and studios</td><td>Home Assistant&#39;s own frontend (dialogs in <code>home-assistant</code>, the card picker, real recorder data)</td></tr><tr><td><a href="#layer-b-visual-baselines">Layer B: visual baselines</a></td><td>local only, <code>npm run test:visual</code></td><td>Pixel baselines of a few canonical cards, to catch unintended visual change</td><td>Behaviour (use Layer A)</td></tr><tr><td><a href="#layer-c-real-home-assistant">Layer C: real Home Assistant</a></td><td>local only, <code>npm run test:ha</code></td><td>The integration, WebSocket commands and permissions, services, Lovelace dashboards, editors inside Home Assistant</td><td>Anything Layer A can already see (Layer C is slow and needs the dev Home Assistant)</td></tr><tr><td><a href="#docs-site-tests">Docs site</a></td><td>CI (without <code>@gpu</code>) and local, <code>npm run test:docs</code></td><td>The built docs: live demos, galleries, playgrounds, the config checker, published files, moved anchors</td><td>Card behaviour (test that in Layer A; the docs tests only check that demos render)</td></tr></tbody></table><p>All four Playwright layers use the built bundle, so run <code>npm run build</code> first (and <code>npm run docs:build</code> for the docs tests).</p><hr><h2 id="node-tests" tabindex="-1">Node Tests <a class="header-anchor" href="#node-tests" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Node Tests&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong><code>node:test</code> suites under <code>tests/</code>, one folder per area.</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Folders:</strong> <code>charts</code>, <code>core</code> (the biggest), <code>data-grid</code>, <code>docs</code>, <code>editor</code>, <code>ha-compat</code>, <code>layout</code>, <code>msd</code>, <code>routing</code> (<code>npm run test:routing</code> runs it alone), <code>support</code>. <code>npm test</code> runs every <code>tests/**/*.test.js</code> (<code>package.json</code>, <code>test</code> script); <code>tests/fixtures/</code> holds shared input files.</li><li><strong>Preloads:</strong> every <code>test*</code> script runs node with two <code>--import</code>s (<code>package.json</code>): <ul><li><code>tests/setup/browser-shims.js</code> gives <code>window</code> (as <code>globalThis</code>), <code>__LCARDS_VERSION__</code> and <code>requestAnimationFrame</code>, which <code>src/</code> modules touch at import time. Deliberately no fake <code>document</code>: Lit fails on a partial one.</li><li><code>tests/setup/vite-imports.js</code> makes the Vite import forms <code>src/</code> uses load in node: <code>import text from &#39;./file?raw&#39;</code> and <code>import data from &#39;./file.json&#39;</code> (only for the repo&#39;s own sources).</li></ul></li><li><strong><code>--no-maglev</code></strong> is on every node script that runs the config checker (the tests, <code>validate-doc-examples</code>, <code>validate:config</code>). V8&#39;s Maglev compiler otherwise spends up to 10 GB optimising ajv&#39;s huge generated validators, and the <code>astro-starters</code> and <code>demo-dashboard</code> tests get killed for memory (COOKBOOK.md, Tests).</li><li><strong>Helpers</strong> in <code>tests/helpers/</code>: <ul><li><code>fake-hass.js</code>: <code>createFakeHass({ states, replies })</code>, a browser-safe <code>hass</code> with a service-call spy (<code>hass.serviceCalls</code>), a scripted connection (<code>hass.connection.sent</code>, <code>replies</code> for <code>sendMessagePromise</code>) and <code>hass.connection.push(id, payload)</code> to deliver a subscription message. The docs harness imports it too, so it has no <code>node:</code> imports.</li><li><code>jsdom-env.js</code>: <code>installJsdom()</code> puts a real jsdom <code>document</code> and window APIs on <code>globalThis</code> for tests that need them (theme activation, a headless <code>lcardsCore.initialize()</code>); <code>env.restore()</code> takes it down.</li><li><code>card-env.js</code> (the card environment shared with the browser harness and the docs demos), <code>source-scan.js</code> (<code>findInSource()</code> for source guard tests), <code>demo-states.js</code>, <code>allowed-entities.js</code>, <code>ha-jinja.js</code>.</li></ul></li><li><strong>Guard tests</strong> read the source instead of running it: colour syntax only in <code>color-syntax.js</code>, no <code>@ts-ignore</code>, known-dead Home Assistant elements (<code>tests/ha-compat/</code>), the schema vocabulary, docs format and the &quot;Home Assistant&quot; spelling (<code>tests/docs/</code>). When one fails, fix the code, not the guard.</li><li><strong>Python from node:</strong> pure Python rules are tested by running the file in <code>python3</code> from a node test, for example <code>tests/support/device-access.test.js</code> for <code>custom_components/lcards/device_access.py</code>.</li></ul><hr><h2 id="layer-a-browser-components" tabindex="-1">Layer A: Browser Components <a class="header-anchor" href="#layer-a-browser-components" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Layer A: Browser Components&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>One real card per fixture, from the built bundle, in Chromium against a fake <code>hass</code></strong> (<code>playwright.config.js</code>, project <code>component</code>; specs <code>tests/browser/*.spec.js</code>).</p><ul><li><strong>Server:</strong> <code>tests/browser/serve.mjs</code> serves <code>custom_components/lcards/</code> at <code>/lcards/</code> (as Home Assistant does, <code>.br</code>/<code>.gz</code> included), <code>tests/helpers/</code> at <code>/helpers/</code>, the fixtures at <code>/fixtures/</code> and the harness at <code>/</code>.</li><li><strong>Fixtures:</strong> <code>tests/browser/fixtures/&lt;case&gt;.yaml</code>, one card config each. <code>mountCase(page, &#39;&lt;case&gt;&#39;, params)</code> from <code>tests/browser/support.js</code> opens <code>harness.html?case=&lt;case&gt;</code> and waits until the card has rendered; <code>expectNoErrors(page)</code> fails on any LCARdS console error.</li><li><strong>Harness</strong> (<code>tests/browser/www/harness.html</code> and <code>harness.js</code>): loads <code>/lcards/lcards.js?v=test</code> like Home Assistant, mounts the card and exposes: <ul><li><code>window.__setState(entityId, state)</code>: pushes a state change (a new <code>hass</code> snapshot plus a <code>state_changed</code> event);</li><li><code>window.__reconfigure(config)</code>: <code>setConfig</code> on the live card;</li><li><code>window.__card</code>, <code>window.__hass</code> (with <code>serviceCalls</code>), <code>window.__events</code> (<code>hass-more-info</code>, <code>show-dialog</code>, <code>location-changed</code>), <code>window.__errors</code>, <code>window.__mountExtra(config)</code>.</li></ul></li><li><strong>Configs are deep-frozen</strong>, as Home Assistant freezes Lovelace configs (<code>deepFreeze()</code> in <code>tests/helpers/card-env.js</code>), so code that mutates its config in place fails here too.</li><li><strong>Harness parameters:</strong> <code>width</code>, <code>height</code>, <code>freeze=1</code> (removes CSS transitions; animations keep running), <code>now=&lt;ms&gt;</code>, <code>seed=&lt;n&gt;</code>, <code>log=&lt;level&gt;</code>, <code>connectFirst=1</code> (attach and give <code>hass</code> before <code>setConfig</code>, the order Home Assistant uses on a live reconfigure), <code>historyDelay=&lt;entity_id&gt;:&lt;ms&gt;</code>.</li><li><strong>Cards that size to 100%</strong> (charts, rings) need a definite size: <code>mountCase(page, &#39;chart-area&#39;, { height: &#39;300&#39; })</code>.</li><li><strong>CI</strong> runs it on every push and PR (Validate workflow, Tests and Typecheck job) with two retries; locally a failure is a failure (<code>playwright.config.js</code>).</li></ul><hr><h2 id="layer-b-visual-baselines" tabindex="-1">Layer B: Visual Baselines <a class="header-anchor" href="#layer-b-visual-baselines" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Layer B: Visual Baselines&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Screenshot baselines of canonical cards</strong> (<code>tests/browser/visual.spec.js</code>, project <code>visual</code>; images in <code>tests/browser/visual.spec.js-snapshots/</code>).</p><ul><li><strong>Local only:</strong> fonts render differently from machine to machine.</li><li><strong>Deterministic input:</strong> each case mounts with <code>freeze=1</code> (no CSS transitions), <code>now=</code> (the sample entity values follow the time of day) and <code>seed=1</code> (<code>Math.random</code> is reseeded just before the card mounts, so generated decoration is stable); chart cases also fix the browser clock for their time axis.</li><li><strong>Tolerance</strong> is an absolute pixel budget (<code>maxDiffPixels: 40</code>, per-pixel <code>threshold: 0.2</code>), because a ratio let a whole missing label pass.</li><li><strong>After an intended visual change</strong> refresh with <code>npm run test:visual -- --update-snapshots=all</code>. The plain <code>--update-snapshots</code> only rewrites baselines that fail, and keeps the ones that still pass within the tolerance. Look at every changed image before committing it.</li></ul><hr><h2 id="layer-c-real-home-assistant" tabindex="-1">Layer C: Real Home Assistant <a class="header-anchor" href="#layer-c-real-home-assistant" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Layer C: Real Home Assistant&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Smoke tests against the dev Home Assistant</strong> (<code>playwright.ha.config.js</code>; <code>tests/ha/ha.spec.js</code>, client <code>tests/ha/ha-client.mjs</code>). Local only, never on CI.</p><ul><li><strong>Needs:</strong> <code>npm run build</code> (the bind-mounted <code>custom_components/lcards/</code> is what Home Assistant serves), the dev Home Assistant at <code>HA_URL</code> (default <code>http://localhost:8123</code>) and a long-lived admin token in <code>$HA_TOKEN</code> or the first line of <code>.ha-token</code> in the repo root (gitignored). Without a token every test skips.</li><li><strong>Fixtures it creates if missing:</strong> only <code>lcards_test_*</code> helpers (<code>TEST_HELPERS</code>; the dashboard gallery&#39;s extra ones are <code>GALLERY_HELPERS</code>) and the <code>lcards-test</code> dashboard, rewritten from <code>tests/ha/dashboard.yaml</code> (<code>ensureHelpers()</code>, <code>ensureDashboard()</code>). Tests use these fixtures, never personal entities, and clean up what else they create (a temporary user, below).</li><li><strong>Non-admin cases:</strong> <code>withNonAdminUser(admin, async (user, userId) =&gt; { ... })</code> creates a temporary non-admin user, logs it in for a token, runs the callback with a client for that user, then deletes the user and its scoped storage. Use it for every permission check (the storage and <code>lcards/fire_event</code> rules on <a href="./backend-api.html">Backend API</a>).</li><li><strong>One worker,</strong> because the tests change shared Home Assistant state; Home Assistant&#39;s service worker is blocked, since it reloads the page a few seconds into a fresh profile.</li></ul><hr><h2 id="docs-site-tests" tabindex="-1">Docs Site Tests <a class="header-anchor" href="#docs-site-tests" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Docs Site Tests&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>The built docs site in Chromium</strong> (<code>playwright.docs.config.js</code>; specs <code>tests/docs-site/</code>, shared helpers <code>tests/docs-site/support.js</code>).</p><ul><li><strong>Two servers:</strong> project <code>built</code> runs every spec against <code>vitepress preview</code> of the built site; project <code>dev</code> runs <code>dev-server.spec.js</code> against <code>vitepress dev</code>, which serves <code>/lcards/</code> differently and once broke the demos there.</li><li><strong>What they cover:</strong> live card demos (<code>live-cards.spec.js</code>), galleries, playgrounds, the processor lab, the demo board, the browser config checker, the published schemas and <code>llms.txt</code> (<code>published-files.spec.js</code>) and moved anchors (<code>moved-anchors.spec.js</code>).</li><li><strong><code>@gpu</code> tags:</strong> pages of many WebGL cards (astrometrics) and a few CPU-heavy pages are tagged with <code>gpuTag(url)</code>; CI has no GPU and skips them (<code>grepInvert: /@gpu/</code>), one astrometrics page stays on CI as a smoke test. Locally they run on the real GPU through four Chromium flags (without them headless Chromium draws WebGL on the CPU).</li><li><strong><code>Accept-Encoding: identity</code>:</strong> <code>vitepress preview</code> brotli-compresses every response at the highest level, uncached, which cost seconds per page for the main chunk; the tests ask for uncompressed responses.</li><li><strong>Channels:</strong> <code>DOCS_BASE</code> (<code>/</code>, <code>/dev/</code>, <code>/v/&lt;version&gt;/</code>) puts every spec path under the channel&#39;s base (<code>underBase()</code> in <code>support.js</code>), so a channel can be tested locally with <code>DOCS_BASE=/dev/ npm run docs:build &amp;&amp; DOCS_BASE=/dev/ npm run test:docs</code> (COOKBOOK.md, Gotchas).</li></ul><hr><h2 id="docs-live-demos" tabindex="-1">Docs Live Demos <a class="header-anchor" href="#docs-live-demos" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Docs Live Demos&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Docs examples are tests too: they render the real cards and are schema-checked.</strong> How to write them is the <a href="https://github.com/snootched/lcards/blob/main/.claude/skills/lcards-docs-writer/SKILL.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><code>lcards-docs-writer</code> skill</a> and <a href="https://github.com/snootched/lcards/blob/main/COOKBOOK.md#docs-site" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">COOKBOOK.md: Docs site</a>.</p><ul><li><strong><code>```yaml live</code></strong> renders a live demo above the YAML, picked by the card&#39;s <code>type:</code> (<code>LIVE_DEMOS</code> in <code>doc/.vitepress/config.mts</code>): <code>CardDemo.vue</code> for button, elbow, slider, select menu, MSD, layout card and view, astrometrics and the alert overlay (on the Alert Studio&#39;s mock screen); <code>ChartDemo.vue</code> and <code>DataGridDemo.vue</code> for charts and data grids. <code>collapsed</code> folds the YAML away, <code>controls</code> adds a control for each entity the YAML names.</li><li><strong><code>&lt;CardGallery set=&quot;...&quot; /&gt;</code></strong> renders a grid of real cards, one per option value (every preset, every elbow type), from the sets in <code>card-demo-galleries.js</code>. Look at a screenshot of a new gallery: the tests check that cards render, not that they look right.</li><li><strong><code>```yaml lab</code></strong> wraps the example in <code>ProcessorLab.vue</code>: the real DataSource processors run over sample history (<code>src/core/data-sources/processor-preview.js</code>).</li><li><strong>Node checks:</strong> every YAML block is validated strictly against the schema in <code>npm run build</code>; <code>tests/docs/card-demo-starters.test.js</code> schema-checks every demo starter and gallery tile, <code>tests/docs/processor-lab.test.js</code> runs every lab block in node, and <code>tests/docs/demo-dashboard.test.js</code> and <code>dashboard-gallery.test.js</code> validate the demo views and gallery dashboards offline.</li><li><strong>Browser checks:</strong> the docs site tests above mount every demo on the built site and fail on any error.</li></ul><hr><h2 id="running-heavy-tests" tabindex="-1">Running Heavy Tests <a class="header-anchor" href="#running-heavy-tests" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Running Heavy Tests&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Run one heavy job at a time:</strong> <code>npm test</code>, <code>npm run typecheck</code>, the doc-example validation, <code>npm run build</code> and every Playwright layer.</p><ul><li>Each one loads the whole bundle, the generated validators or a fleet of browsers; two at once on a laptop already running the dev Home Assistant ran out of memory.</li><li>A Playwright failure that only shows in a full run and passes alone is usually machine load (the Home Assistant container, the editor, other browsers), not a bug: rerun that spec alone before chasing it.</li></ul><hr><h2 id="see-also" tabindex="-1">See Also <a class="header-anchor" href="#see-also" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;See Also&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><a href="./typescript.html">Type Checking</a>: <code>npm run typecheck</code>, the other gate that runs on CI</li><li><a href="./debug-api.html">Debug API</a>: the console tools for finding what a test should check</li><li><a href="./backend-api.html">Backend API</a>: the commands and permission rules the Layer C tests cover</li></ul>',38)])])}const p=o(d,[["render",r]]);export{m as __pageData,p as default};
